{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.822d80630e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-319","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Pembrook Bridge is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1987.</li><li>Its main span is 909 meters.</li><li>The Sable Bridge is in western Kenya. It opened in 1927.</li><li>Its main span is 463 meters.</li><li>Both are arch bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Pembrook Bridge and the Sable Bridge.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The main span of the Pembrook Bridge (909 meters) is greater than that of the Sable Bridge (463 meters).","Both the Pembrook Bridge and the Sable Bridge are arch bridges.","The Pembrook Bridge is in southern Brazil, and the Sable Bridge is in western Kenya.","The Pembrook Bridge opened in 1987, while the Sable Bridge opened in 1927."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e46b3865b0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-323","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Valewood National Park is in southern Brazil. It was established in 1894.</li><li>Its area is 6,025 square kilometers.</li><li>Fenwick National Park is in eastern Poland. It was established in 1998.</li><li>Its area is 5,087 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks that protect coastal wetlands.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Valewood National Park and Fenwick National Park.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Valewood National Park and Fenwick National Park are parks that protect coastal wetlands.","The area of Valewood National Park (6,025 square kilometers) is greater than that of Fenwick National Park (5,087 square kilometers).","Valewood National Park was established in 1894, while Fenwick National Park was established in 1998.","Valewood National Park is in southern Brazil, and Fenwick National Park is in eastern Poland."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.dfa80740fb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-325","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Ironwood Museum is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1973.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 668,191 visitors.</li><li>The Brookhaven Museum is in central Canada. It opened in 1935.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 418,502 visitors.</li><li>Both are museums of natural history.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two museums opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Ironwood Museum opened in 1973, while the Brookhaven Museum opened in 1935.","Both the Ironwood Museum and the Brookhaven Museum are museums of natural history.","The annual attendance of the Ironwood Museum (668,191 visitors) is greater than that of the Brookhaven Museum (418,502 visitors).","The Ironwood Museum is in southern Brazil, and the Brookhaven Museum is in central Canada."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1cd900ca5e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-335","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Eastwick Tunnel is in central Canada. It opened in 1978.</li><li>Its length is 43 kilometers.</li><li>The Larkspur Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 2001.</li><li>Its length is 16 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two tunnels opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Eastwick Tunnel is in central Canada, and the Larkspur Tunnel is in southern Norway.","Both the Eastwick Tunnel and the Larkspur Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The length of the Eastwick Tunnel (43 kilometers) is greater than that of the Larkspur Tunnel (16 kilometers).","The Eastwick Tunnel opened in 1978, while the Larkspur Tunnel opened in 2001."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bfa1bafee4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-336","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Juniper is in western Kenya. It was first climbed in 1892.</li><li>Its height is 6,115 meters.</li><li>Mount Cresthill is in eastern Australia. It was first climbed in 1964.</li><li>Its height is 5,586 meters.</li><li>Both are peaks in the same mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of Mount Juniper (6,115 meters) is greater than that of Mount Cresthill (5,586 meters).","Mount Juniper was first climbed in 1892, while Mount Cresthill was first climbed in 1964.","Mount Juniper is in western Kenya, and Mount Cresthill is in eastern Australia.","Both Mount Juniper and Mount Cresthill are peaks in the same mountain range."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.798add8479","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-341","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Northam Tunnel is in western Scotland. It opened in 1924.</li><li>Its length is 45 kilometers.</li><li>The Halden Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1894.</li><li>Its length is 23 kilometers.</li><li>Both are railway tunnels.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the length of the two tunnels differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Northam Tunnel opened in 1924, while the Halden Tunnel opened in 1894.","Both the Northam Tunnel and the Halden Tunnel are railway tunnels.","The Northam Tunnel, which is in western Scotland, is one of several railway tunnels.","The length of the Northam Tunnel (45 kilometers) is greater than that of the Halden Tunnel (23 kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the length of both tunnels. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tunnel."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.adf13e88cd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-347","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Valewood Tower is in northern Chile. It was completed in 1924.</li><li>Its height is 287 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Tower is in southern Brazil. It was completed in 2013.</li><li>Its height is 166 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two towers were completed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Valewood Tower was completed in 1924, while the Cresthill Tower was completed in 2013.","The height of the Valewood Tower (287 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Tower (166 meters).","The Valewood Tower is in northern Chile, and the Cresthill Tower is in southern Brazil.","Both the Valewood Tower and the Cresthill Tower are observation towers open to the public."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.23db4e37bd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-358","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Orla is in central Canada. It was first climbed in 1985.</li><li>Its height is 5,580 meters.</li><li>Mount Thornbury is in eastern Poland. It was first climbed in 1893.</li><li>Its height is 3,193 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Mount Orla and Mount Thornbury are dormant volcanoes.","Mount Orla is in central Canada, and Mount Thornbury is in eastern Poland.","The height of Mount Orla (5,580 meters) is greater than that of Mount Thornbury (3,193 meters).","Mount Orla was first climbed in 1985, while Mount Thornbury was first climbed in 1893."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.db019170eb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-376","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Alder Museum is in eastern Poland. It opened in 1944.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 493,418 visitors.</li><li>The Merrow Museum is in northern Chile. It opened in 2016.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 442,176 visitors.</li><li>Both are art museums with free admission.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Alder Museum and the Merrow Museum.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Alder Museum is in eastern Poland, and the Merrow Museum is in northern Chile.","The annual attendance of the Alder Museum (493,418 visitors) is greater than that of the Merrow Museum (442,176 visitors).","The Alder Museum opened in 1944, while the Merrow Museum opened in 2016.","Both the Alder Museum and the Merrow Museum are art museums with free admission."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7aebd647a3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-380","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Orla Observatory is in northern India. It began operating in 1966.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia. It began operating in 2010.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 7 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two observatorys began operating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Orla Observatory began operating in 1966, while the Cresthill Observatory began operating in 2010.","The Orla Observatory is in northern India, and the Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Orla Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Observatory (7 meters).","Both the Orla Observatory and the Cresthill Observatory are radio observatories."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2b1a1c146e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-385","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Wexford Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1928.</li><li>Its height is 348 meters.</li><li>The Northam Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1925.</li><li>Its height is 195 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two towers differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Wexford Tower (348 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Tower (195 meters).","Both the Wexford Tower and the Northam Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The Wexford Tower was completed in 1928, while the Northam Tower was completed in 1925.","The Wexford Tower, which is in central Canada, is one of several towers built mainly of steel."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both towers. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tower."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c1f3f07438","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-390","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Valewood Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1999.</li><li>Its length is 11 kilometers.</li><li>The Eastwick Tunnel is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 2011.</li><li>Its length is 5 kilometers.</li><li>Both are railway tunnels.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Valewood Tunnel and the Eastwick Tunnel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The length of the Valewood Tunnel (11 kilometers) is greater than that of the Eastwick Tunnel (5 kilometers).","The Valewood Tunnel is in southern Norway, and the Eastwick Tunnel is in southern New Zealand.","The Valewood Tunnel opened in 1999, while the Eastwick Tunnel opened in 2011.","Both the Valewood Tunnel and the Eastwick Tunnel are railway tunnels."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.71d830cd0b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-10","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Iyer is an ecologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Iyer compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Iyer found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Iyer concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Iyer concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","To investigate the question, Iyer compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Ecologist Kofi Iyer set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.","Iyer found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0528f3903d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-14","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Novak is a psychologist.</li><li>Novak wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","Psychologist Elias Novak set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d09004f165","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-19","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Halvorsen is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Halvorsen grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Halvorsen found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Agricultural scientist Zara Halvorsen set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","Halvorsen concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.","Halvorsen found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bb0f08b1dd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-22","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Takeda is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Takeda grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Takeda found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Agricultural scientist Priya Takeda set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","Takeda found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.","To investigate the question, Takeda grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.951edc99d6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-34","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Takeda is an entomologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Takeda set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Takeda found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.","Entomologist Tomás Takeda set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.","To investigate the question, Takeda set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2b5e828717","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-49","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Asante is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Asante asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Asante found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Asante concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Asante asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Nutrition researcher Chen Asante set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.","Asante found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","Asante concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1befb3c560","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-49","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Asante is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Asante asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Asante found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Asante concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Asante asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Asante concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Asante found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","Nutrition researcher Chen Asante set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3f8c1d5f20","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-56","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Haddad is a zoologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Zoologist Beatriz Haddad set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","To investigate the question, Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0617657af5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-58","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Iyer is a zoologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Iyer gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Iyer found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Iyer concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Zoologist Diego Iyer set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Iyer concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Iyer found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","To investigate the question, Iyer gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.093ed747aa","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-68","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Moreau is a linguist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Moreau gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Moreau found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Moreau concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","To investigate the question, Moreau gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Moreau found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","Linguist Tomás Moreau set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.00b62b1b29","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-74","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Moreau is an economist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Moreau tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Moreau found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Moreau concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","To investigate the question, Moreau tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.","Economist Felipe Moreau set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.","Moreau found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a1ac2bbf62","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-81","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Mensah is a botanist.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Mensah grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Mensah found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Mensah concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Botanist Viktor Mensah set out to learn whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.","Mensah found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","Mensah concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.","To investigate the question, Mensah grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1a68f0d4bd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-85","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Imani Oyelaran is an urban planner.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","To investigate the question, Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.","Urban planner Imani Oyelaran set out to learn whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.","Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f5a81d7d4e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-112","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Samuel Adeyemi is a primatologist.</li><li>Adeyemi wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Adeyemi observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Adeyemi found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Adeyemi concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Adeyemi concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","Primatologist Samuel Adeyemi set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.","To investigate the question, Adeyemi observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Adeyemi found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2a4a5533fc","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-116","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Ferreira is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Ferreira asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Ferreira found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sleep researcher Tomás Ferreira set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.","To investigate the question, Ferreira asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Ferreira concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","Ferreira found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.5d48dcb649","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-117","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Rahman is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Rahman asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Rahman found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Rahman concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sleep researcher Elias Rahman set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.","To investigate the question, Rahman asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Rahman concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","Rahman found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d5c50ebcac","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-136","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Mbeki is an archaeologist.</li><li>Mbeki wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Mbeki analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Mbeki found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Mbeki concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mbeki found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Mbeki concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","To investigate the question, Mbeki analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Archaeologist Diego Mbeki set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.73bdb14784","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-149","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Kowalski is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Kowalski fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Kowalski found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Kowalski fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Kowalski found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Marine ecologist Sofia Kowalski set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.eaab068d00","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-153","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Idris Nakamura is a climate scientist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Nakamura compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Nakamura found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","Nakamura concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Climate scientist Idris Nakamura set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","To investigate the question, Nakamura compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8f42e3fb67","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-158","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Nakamura is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Nakamura moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Nakamura found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Behavioral economist Yara Nakamura set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","To investigate the question, Nakamura moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Nakamura concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","Nakamura found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.dc5cc6c095","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-161","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Solberg is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Solberg wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Solberg moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Solberg found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Solberg concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Solberg moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Behavioral economist Jonas Solberg set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","Solberg found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","Solberg concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.20114be9d7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-164","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Kowalski is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Kowalski played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Kowalski found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kowalski found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Veterinary scientist Lucía Kowalski set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","To investigate the question, Kowalski played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.db59350bf2","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-172","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Oyelaran is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Oyelaran added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Oyelaran found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Oyelaran concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Oyelaran added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Forest ecologist Kenji Oyelaran set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients.","Oyelaran found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.365c596b9d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-174","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Okafor is a music researcher.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Okafor tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Okafor found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Okafor tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Music researcher Sofia Okafor set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Okafor found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."}
